INT. COURTROOM, MARITIME LAW DIVISION - DAY

Dark oak. Brass fittings. A model of a container ship sits on the evidence table, its cranes frozen mid-lift. Salt-water stains the carpet near the jury box, a leak the clerk has long stopped noticing.

JUDGE HORTENSE ABELARD-OKONKWO (60s, half-moon glasses, voice like creaking rope) presides. At the plaintiff's table stands attorney PERCIVAL GRIMSDYKE (50s, pinstripe, cane) with a thick binder. Across the aisle, defense counsel BEATRIX NAKAMURA-FLYNN (40s, severe bun, red pen) leans casually on her chair.

JUDGE ABELARD-OKONKWO
Mr. Grimsdyke, you may proceed with your closing.

GRIMSDYKE
Thank you, Your Honor.

He rises slowly, leaning on the cane. He addresses the empty jury box as though it were full.

GRIMSDYKE (CONT'D)
(to the gallery)
Ladies and gentlemen, this case is about a ship. It is also about a promise.

NAKAMURA-FLYNN
(muttering)
It's about a bill of lading.

GRIMSDYKE
The Meridian Star sailed from Rotterdam with four thousand containers. Four thousand promises. Not one was delivered intact.

JUDGE ABELARD-OKONKWO
Mr. Grimsdyke, I hope you're going somewhere with this.

GRIMSDYKE
(to the Judge)
Yes, Your Honor.

He lifts the model ship from the table and turns it in his hands.

GRIMSDYKE (CONT'D)
My client alleges that Ms. Nakamura-Flynn's client knowingly loaded cargo in violation of Article Nine.

NAKAMURA-FLYNN
Objection. Speculation.

JUDGE ABELARD-OKONKWO
Sustained. Rephrase.

GRIMSDYKE
My client alleges that the knowledge was there.

NAKAMURA-FLYNN
(standing)
Objection. Still speculation.

JUDGE ABELARD-OKONKWO
Overruled. I'm curious.

Nakamura-Flynn sits, tapping her pen.

GRIMSDYKE
(to himself)
Finally.

He sets the model down gently. It tips on its stand, a crane snaps off, and the sound ricochets around the silent room.

GRIMSDYKE (CONT'D)
(to the gallery)
Four thousand promises, ladies and gentlemen. And a clerk who never fixed the leak.

JUDGE ABELARD-OKONKWO
Mr. Grimsdyke.

GRIMSDYKE
Your Honor?

JUDGE ABELARD-OKONKWO
That's enough.

He bows, retrieves his cane, and sits. Nakamura-Flynn rises, buttons her jacket, and walks calmly to the evidence table. She picks up the broken crane and weighs it in her palm.

NAKAMURA-FLYNN
(to the Judge)
Your Honor, my learned friend has just proven my case.

JUDGE ABELARD-OKONKWO
Do enlighten us.

NAKAMURA-FLYNN
(to the room)
A crane cannot be loaded wrong. It can only be broken. And broken things get insured.

Grimsdyke closes his eyes. The judge exhales through her nose, and removes her glasses.

JUDGE ABELARD-OKONKWO
We will recess for lunch.
